+        broadcast to every worker, which slices its own shard internally.
+
+        ``bucket_sizes`` is the number of tensors per broadcast bucket, exactly as the
+        sender grouped them. Each bucket is staged on device, received, then moved to
+        host memory and released, so peak device memory is ONE bucket instead of the
+        whole adapter: a MoE adapter runs to several GB, and allocating that on top of
+        the KV cache OOMs mid-broadcast, which wedges every other participant until the
+        NCCL timeout. Host tensors are what ``LoRAAdapter`` keeps anyway
